DSP.mod(3,{"m3-numpy":{"id":"m3-numpy","slug":"numpy-essentials-and-broadcasting","m":3,"title":"NumPy essentials and broadcasting","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m1-checklist"],"tags":["numpy","python","arrays"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"NumPy arrays, shapes and axes, indexing, broadcasting rules and why vectorised code is faster than Python loops.","keyTerms":["ndarray","shape","dtype","broadcasting","vectorization","boolean mask","axis","reshape"],"interviewQs":["What are the broadcasting rules? Give an example.","What does axis=0 mean in sum(axis=0)?","Why is a NumPy operation faster than a Python loop?"],"mistakes":["Confusing axis=0 and axis=1","Mutating a view and expecting a copy","Using a Python loop over a NumPy array"],"practice":"Standardise each column of a random matrix using broadcasting, without loops, and verify the means are about 0.","related":["m3-pandas-checklist","m7-optimization"],"points":"- ndarray creation: array, zeros, ones, arange, linspace, eye, random\n- Shape, dtype, reshape, ravel, flatten, transpose, newaxis\n- Indexing, slicing, boolean masking, fancy indexing\n- Broadcasting rules (dimensions compared from the right; each pair must be equal or one must be 1)\n- Vectorization vs Python loops (and why it is faster)\n- Axis operations: sum(axis=0) collapses rows (column-wise result), axis=1 collapses columns\n- np.where, np.argmax, np.argsort, np.unique, np.percentile, np.corrcoef\n- Linear algebra: @, dot, np.linalg.inv, eig, norm"},"intuitive":"<p>A NumPy array is a neat grid of numbers stored in one block of memory, like a spreadsheet that does maths on the whole grid at once. Instead of telling Python &quot;take the first number, add 1, then the second...&quot; you just say &quot;add 1 to everything&quot; and it happens in fast compiled code. Broadcasting is the polite rule that lets a small grid stretch to match a bigger one: adding a row of three numbers to a table with three columns adds it to every row.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Everything in pandas, scikit-learn and deep learning sits on NumPy; vectorised thinking is expected in interviews.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Think in whole arrays, know which axis collapses, and remember broadcasting compares shapes from the right.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;NumPy stores numbers in one typed block of memory and applies operations to the whole array at once, which is why it is fast: no Python loop per element. Broadcasting is the rule that lets arrays of different shapes combine. Compare the shapes from the right; two dimensions match if they are equal or one of them is 1, and the 1 is stretched. So a 3 by 4 array minus a length-4 row of column means works, one value per column. To subtract row means I need shape 3 by 1, so I use keepdims or reshape. Slices are views, not copies, so editing one edits the original. The catch: axis zero is down the rows, collapsing rows, and axis one is across columns.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Broadcasting, traced on a small score table","html":"<p>Three students (rows) and three subjects (columns):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Maths</th><th>Physics</th><th>Chemistry</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asha</td><td>60</td><td>70</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>Ravi</td><td>80</td><td>90</td><td>70</td></tr><tr><td>Meera</td><td>70</td><td>80</td><td>90</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">S = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">60</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">70</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">80</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">80</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">90</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">70</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">70</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">80</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">90</span>]])</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Column means</strong> (<code>axis=0</code> collapses the rows, leaving one number per subject): <code>S.mean(axis=0)</code> = [70, 80, 80]. Shape (3,).</li>\n<li><strong>Subtract them.</strong> <code>S - S.mean(axis=0)</code> lines up shapes from the right: (3, 3) against (3,). The row of three means is copied down every row, so each column is centred on its own subject:\n[[−10, −10, 0], [10, 10, −10], [0, 0, 10]]. Ravi is 10 above the class in Maths.</li>\n<li><strong>Row means</strong> (<code>axis=1</code> collapses the columns): Asha 70, Ravi 80, Meera 80, shape (3,).</li>\n<li><strong>The trap.</strong> <code>S - S.mean(axis=1)</code> <em>also</em> runs, because the shape (3,) still lines up with the last axis. But it subtracts Asha&#39;s mean from the Maths column, Ravi&#39;s from Physics and so on. Wrong, silently.</li>\n<li><strong>The fix.</strong> <code>S.mean(axis=1, keepdims=True)</code> has shape (3, 1): one number per row, standing as a column. Broadcasting stretches it across: [[−10, 0, 10], [0, 10, −10], [−10, 0, 10]]. Each student is now compared with their own average.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Read shapes from the right; a 1 stretches. With a square table, the wrong axis does not even raise an error, so always ask &quot;one number per row, or per column?&quot;</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-creating-arrays-and-reading-their-shape","text":"Creating arrays and reading their shape","level":3},{"id":"h-indexing-slicing-masks-and-fancy-indexing","text":"Indexing, slicing, masks and fancy indexing","level":3},{"id":"h-axes-the-rule-that-stops-the-confusion","text":"Axes: the rule that stops the confusion","level":3},{"id":"h-broadcasting-compare-shapes-from-the-right","text":"Broadcasting: compare shapes from the right","level":3},{"id":"h-vectorisation-why-loops-lose","text":"Vectorisation: why loops lose","level":3},{"id":"h-handy-functions","text":"Handy functions","level":3},{"id":"h-linear-algebra-you-will-actually-use","text":"Linear algebra you will actually use","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"cl","id":"m3-numpy.c1","kind":"list","items":["<code>ndarray</code> creation: <code>array</code>, <code>zeros</code>, <code>ones</code>, <code>arange</code>, <code>linspace</code>, <code>eye</code>, <code>random</code>","Shape, dtype, <code>reshape</code>, <code>ravel</code>, <code>flatten</code>, <code>transpose</code>, <code>newaxis</code>","Indexing, slicing, boolean masking, fancy indexing","<strong>Broadcasting rules</strong> (dimensions compared from the right; each pair must be equal or one must be 1)","Vectorization vs Python loops (and why it is faster)","Axis operations: <code>sum(axis=0)</code> collapses rows (column-wise result), <code>axis=1</code> collapses columns","<code>np.where</code>, <code>np.argmax</code>, <code>np.argsort</code>, <code>np.unique</code>, <code>np.percentile</code>, <code>np.corrcoef</code>","Linear algebra: <code>@</code>, <code>dot</code>, <code>np.linalg.inv</code>, <code>eig</code>, <code>norm</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nX = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>]])\nX.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [2.5, 3.5, 4.5]  per column</span>\nX - X.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># broadcasting: center columns</span>\nX[X &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># boolean mask</span>\nnp.where(X &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># conditional</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-creating-arrays-and-reading-their-shape\">Creating arrays and reading their shape</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n\na = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>]])\na.shape, a.ndim, a.dtype        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ((2, 3), 2, dtype(&#x27;int64&#x27;))</span>\nnp.zeros((<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)), np.ones(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), np.eye(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># filled arrays, identity matrix</span>\nnp.arange(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [0 2 4 6 8]       like range, but an array</span>\nnp.linspace(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>)            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [0.   0.25 0.5  0.75 1.  ]   5 evenly spaced points, both ends included</span>\nnp.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).normal(size=<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># modern random generator, reproducible with a seed</span>\na.reshape(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>), a.reshape(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># -1 means &quot;work it out&quot;; a.ravel() / a.flatten() give the flat version</span>\na.T                             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># transpose: shape (3, 2)</span>\na[:, np.newaxis].shape          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (2, 1, 3)   add an axis of length 1</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-indexing-slicing-masks-and-fancy-indexing\">Indexing, slicing, masks and fancy indexing</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">a[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2               row 0, column 1</span>\na[:, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [2 5]           every row, column 1</span>\na[a &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [3 4 5 6]       boolean mask: keep where True</span>\na[[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]]   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [3 4]           fancy indexing: pairs (0,2) and (1,0)</span>\nnp.where(a &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [[0 0 0] [1 1 1]]   vectorised if/else</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Basic slices return <strong>views</strong> (they share memory with the original); masks and fancy indexing return <strong>copies</strong>. So <code>b = a[:, 0]; b[0] = 99</code> changes <code>a</code>, but <code>c = a[a &gt; 2]; c[0] = 99</code> does not.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-axes-the-rule-that-stops-the-confusion\">Axes: the rule that stops the confusion</h3>\n<p><code>axis</code> names the dimension that <strong>disappears</strong>. For a (2, 3) array, <code>a.sum(axis=0)</code> collapses the rows and returns one value per column (shape (3,)); <code>a.sum(axis=1)</code> collapses the columns and returns one value per row (shape (2,)).</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">a.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [5 7 9]   column totals</span>\na.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [ 6 15]   row totals</span>\na.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [2.5 3.5 4.5]</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-broadcasting-compare-shapes-from-the-right\">Broadcasting: compare shapes from the right</h3>\n<p>Two shapes are compatible when, going from the <strong>last</strong> dimension to the first, each pair of sizes is equal or one of them is 1 (a missing dimension counts as 1). The size-1 side is stretched.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">X = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>]])       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># shape (2, 3)</span>\nX - X.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (2, 3) - (3,)  -&gt; centre each column</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [[-1.5 -1.5 -1.5]</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#  [ 1.5  1.5  1.5]]</span>\n\n(np.ones((<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)) + np.arange(<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>)).shape      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (3, 1) + (4,) -&gt; (3, 4)</span>\nnp.ones((<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)) + np.ones((<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,))             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ValueError: trailing sizes 3 and 2 do not match</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Try shapes in the explorer:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"broadcast","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-vectorisation-why-loops-lose\">Vectorisation: why loops lose</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">x = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).normal(size=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1_000_000</span>)\n\ntotal = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.0</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> v <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> x: total += v * v                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Python loop: one interpreter step per element</span>\ntotal = (x * x).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()                       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># vectorised: one C loop, SIMD-friendly, typically 50x or more faster</span>\ntotal = x @ x                               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># the dot product: same result, often faster still</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Reasons: NumPy stores raw numbers contiguously (no Python object per element), does type checks once per array instead of once per element, and runs the loop in compiled C.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-handy-functions\">Handy functions</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">np.argmax([<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]), np.argsort([<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (1, array([2, 0, 1]))  index of max; indices that would sort</span>\nnp.unique([<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>], return_counts=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (array([1, 2, 3]), array([1, 1, 2]))</span>\nnp.percentile([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">75</span>])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [2. 3. 4.]</span>\nnp.corrcoef([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>])                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2x2 correlation matrix</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-linear-algebra-you-will-actually-use\">Linear algebra you will actually use</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">A = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3.</span>]])\nb = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2.</span>])\nA @ b                         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># matrix-vector product: [4. 7.]</span>\nnp.linalg.solve(A, b)         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># solve A x = b (prefer this to inv(A) @ b)</span>\nvals, vecs = np.linalg.eig(A) <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># eigenvalues and eigenvectors (used in PCA)</span>\nnp.linalg.norm([<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>])        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 5.0   the L2 norm</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-numpy~1","q":"What is the shape of <code>np.ones((4, 1, 3)) + np.ones((5, 1))</code>, or does it fail?","a":"<p>Write the shapes right-aligned, padding the shorter with 1s on the left: <code>(4, 1, 3)</code> and <code>(1, 5, 1)</code>. Compare each pair: 3 and 1 give 3; 1 and 5 give 5; 4 and 1 give 4. The result shape is <code>(4, 5, 3)</code>. It would fail if a pair were different and neither were 1 (for example 3 and 2).</p>\n","s":"(4, 5, 3): align from the right, <code>(4,1,3)</code> with <code>(1,5,1)</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-numpy~2","q":"Standardise every column of a matrix <code>X</code> (mean 0, standard deviation 1) in one line.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">Z = (X - X.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)) / X.std(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>X.mean(axis=0)</code> has shape <code>(d,)</code>; broadcasting subtracts it from every row. Fit the mean and std on the training set only, and reuse them on the test set. For a constant column, <code>std = 0</code> would divide by zero, so guard with <code>np.where(std == 0, 1, std)</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>(X - X.mean(axis=0)) / X.std(axis=0)</code>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-numpy~ca0_1","q":"X has shape (5, 3). How do you centre each column, and what shape is the mean?","a":"<p><code>X - X.mean(axis=0)</code>: the mean has shape (3,), broadcast across all 5 rows.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-numpy~ca0_2","q":"Why does <code>X - X.mean(axis=1)</code> fail for shape (5, 3), and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The mean has shape (5,), which cannot align with the last axis of size 3. Use <code>keepdims=True</code> to get (5, 1).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-numpy~ca0_3","q":"You slice <code>b = a[:2]</code> and set <code>b[0] = 99</code>. What happens to <code>a</code>?","a":"<p><code>a[0]</code> also becomes 99: a basic slice is a view of the same memory. Use <code>.copy()</code> to avoid it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-numpy~ta1_1","q":"<p><code>X</code> has shape (4, 3). What happens with (a) <code>X - X.mean(axis=0)</code>, (b) <code>X - X.mean(axis=1)</code>, (c) <code>X - X.mean(axis=1, keepdims=True)</code>? Give the shape of the mean in each case.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) The mean has shape (3,), which matches the last axis of (4, 3), so it works: each column is centred. (b) The mean has shape (4,), which does not match 3: broadcasting raises a ValueError. (c) The mean has shape (4, 1), which stretches across the 3 columns, so each row is centred. Unlike the square example, a non-square shape makes the mistake in (b) fail loudly.</p>\n"}]},"m3-pandas-checklist":{"id":"m3-pandas-checklist","slug":"pandas-checklist","m":3,"title":"pandas checklist","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m3-numpy"],"tags":["pandas","python","dataframe"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"The pandas operations interviewers expect: reading and inspecting data, selecting, transforming, aggregating, joining, reshaping and time-based operations, with verified examples.","keyTerms":["DataFrame","loc","iloc","boolean filtering","groupby","merge","pivot","rolling","resample","to_datetime"],"interviewQs":["loc vs iloc?","How do you handle missing values in pandas?","Explain groupby with agg vs transform."],"mistakes":["Chained assignment","Using apply row by row when a vectorised method exists","Forgetting to sort before shift or diff"],"practice":"Load any CSV and answer five business questions using only groupby, merge and pivot_table, without loops.","related":["m3-numpy","m3-sql-to-pandas","m3-pandas-problems"],"points":"I/O and inspection\n\n- read_csv (dtype, parse_dates, usecols, chunksize), read_excel, read_json, read_parquet, read_sql\n- head, info, describe, dtypes, shape, isna().sum(), nunique, value_counts\n\nSelection\n\n- loc (label-based, inclusive) vs iloc (position-based, exclusive end)\n- Boolean filtering, query, isin, between, str.contains\n- Avoid chained assignment; understand SettingWithCopyWarning and .copy()\n\nTransformation\n\n- assign, apply (row/column), map, applymap/map on DataFrame, vectorized alternatives\n- astype, to_datetime, .dt accessor, .str accessor\n- cut vs qcut, np.select, np.where\n- fillna, dropna, drop_duplicates, duplicated, replace, clip\n\nAggregation and reshaping\n\n- groupby + agg (named aggregation), transform, filter, apply\n- merge (how=inner/left/right/outer, on, left_on/right_on, suffixes, indicator), join, concat\n- pivot, pivot_table, melt, stack/unstack, crosstab\n- sort_values, nlargest, rank, cumsum, cumcount, shift, diff, pct_change\n- rolling, expanding, ewm, resample\n\nPerformance\n\n- Use categorical dtype, downcast numerics, read in chunks, avoid iterrows, prefer vectorization\n- Parquet over CSV; know Polars/DuckDB exist"},"intuitive":"<p>pandas is a spreadsheet you control with code. A DataFrame is a table with labelled columns; a Series is one column. You pick rows and columns (loc and iloc), filter them with yes/no tests, add new columns, group rows into piles to summarise them (groupby), glue tables together (merge), and rotate tables (pivot). If you already know SQL, almost every idea has a twin in pandas.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> pandas questions are heavily tested in technical rounds, often as &quot;translate this SQL&quot; or &quot;clean this data&quot;.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Select, filter, transform, group, join, reshape, and prefer vectorised methods over loops.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;In pandas I follow a fixed routine. Load, then look at shape, dtypes, head, and missing counts. Select with loc by label or iloc by position, and filter with boolean masks; combine conditions with ampersand and brackets. Create columns by assignment, aggregate with groupby then agg, combine tables with merge, and reshape with pivot or melt. The two groupby tools are agg, which shrinks each group to one row, and transform, which returns a value per original row, like a group mean next to each row. The catch: chained assignment can silently modify a copy, so I use loc to set values, and I always check dtypes, because numbers stored as strings break everything.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"agg versus transform, traced","html":"<p>Five orders:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>row</th><th>user</th><th>amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>a</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>b</td><td>50</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>a</td><td>300</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>c</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>b</td><td>150</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Split</strong> by user: a = {100, 300}, b = {50, 150}, c = {80}.</li>\n<li><strong>agg collapses each group to one row.</strong> <code>df.groupby(&quot;user&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].agg(&quot;sum&quot;)</code> gives a 400, b 200, c 80: three rows, one per user. Use it for &quot;total per user&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>transform hands the group value back to every row.</strong> <code>df.groupby(&quot;user&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].transform(&quot;sum&quot;)</code> gives 400, 200, 400, 80, 200: five rows, aligned with the original table.</li>\n<li><strong>Use transform for per-row comparisons.</strong> <code>df[&quot;share&quot;] = df[&quot;amount&quot;] / df.groupby(&quot;user&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].transform(&quot;sum&quot;)</code> gives 0.25, 0.25, 0.75, 1.00, 0.75. Each order&#39;s share of its user&#39;s total, with no merge needed.</li>\n<li><strong>The SQL equivalents.</strong> agg is <code>GROUP BY</code>; transform is <code>SUM(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user)</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Decide the output shape first. One row per group: agg. Every original row plus a group value: transform.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-reading-and-inspecting","text":"Reading and inspecting","level":3},{"id":"h-selecting-loc-vs-iloc","text":"Selecting: loc vs iloc","level":3},{"id":"h-creating-and-transforming-columns","text":"Creating and transforming columns","level":3},{"id":"h-missing-data-and-duplicates","text":"Missing data and duplicates","level":3},{"id":"h-aggregation-agg-transform-filter","text":"Aggregation: agg, transform, filter","level":3},{"id":"h-joining-and-stacking","text":"Joining and stacking","level":3},{"id":"h-reshaping","text":"Reshaping","level":3},{"id":"h-sequences-and-time","text":"Sequences and time","level":3},{"id":"h-performance-habits","text":"Performance habits","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>I/O and inspection</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-pandas-checklist.c1","kind":"list","items":["<code>read_csv</code> (dtype, parse_dates, usecols, chunksize), <code>read_excel</code>, <code>read_json</code>, <code>read_parquet</code>, <code>read_sql</code>","<code>head</code>, <code>info</code>, <code>describe</code>, <code>dtypes</code>, <code>shape</code>, <code>isna().sum()</code>, <code>nunique</code>, <code>value_counts</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Selection</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-pandas-checklist.c2","kind":"list","items":["<code>loc</code> (label-based, inclusive) vs <code>iloc</code> (position-based, exclusive end)","Boolean filtering, <code>query</code>, <code>isin</code>, <code>between</code>, <code>str.contains</code>","Avoid chained assignment; understand <code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code> and <code>.copy()</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Transformation</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-pandas-checklist.c3","kind":"list","items":["<code>assign</code>, <code>apply</code> (row/column), <code>map</code>, <code>applymap</code>/<code>map</code> on DataFrame, vectorized alternatives","<code>astype</code>, <code>to_datetime</code>, <code>.dt</code> accessor, <code>.str</code> accessor","<code>cut</code> vs <code>qcut</code>, <code>np.select</code>, <code>np.where</code>","<code>fillna</code>, <code>dropna</code>, <code>drop_duplicates</code>, <code>duplicated</code>, <code>replace</code>, <code>clip</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Aggregation and reshaping</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-pandas-checklist.c4","kind":"list","items":["<code>groupby</code> + <code>agg</code> (named aggregation), <code>transform</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>apply</code>","<code>merge</code> (how=inner/left/right/outer, on, left_on/right_on, suffixes, indicator), <code>join</code>, <code>concat</code>","<code>pivot</code>, <code>pivot_table</code>, <code>melt</code>, <code>stack/unstack</code>, <code>crosstab</code>","<code>sort_values</code>, <code>nlargest</code>, <code>rank</code>, <code>cumsum</code>, <code>cumcount</code>, <code>shift</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>pct_change</code>","<code>rolling</code>, <code>expanding</code>, <code>ewm</code>, <code>resample</code>"]},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Performance</strong></p>\n"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-pandas-checklist.c5","kind":"list","items":["Use categorical dtype, downcast numerics, read in chunks, avoid <code>iterrows</code>, prefer vectorization","Parquet over CSV; know Polars/DuckDB exist"]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>The running example is the <code>orders</code> table from the SQL module as a DataFrame:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> pandas <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> pd, numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\norders = pd.DataFrame({\n    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>: <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">17</span>),\n    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>],\n    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>: pd.to_datetime([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-05&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-20&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-06&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-15&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-03&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-12&quot;</span>,\n                                  <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-10&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-14&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-05&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-20&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-02&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-05&quot;</span>,\n                                  <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-09&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-15&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-18&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-03-20&quot;</span>]),\n    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">800</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">400</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1200</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">600</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">900</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1800</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">700</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2200</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">300</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1100</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2600</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1400</span>],\n    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span>]*<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span> + [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cancelled&quot;</span>] + [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span>]*<span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>,\n})</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-reading-and-inspecting\">Reading and inspecting</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df = pd.read_csv(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;orders.csv&quot;</span>, usecols=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>], dtype={<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>: <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;int32&quot;</span>},\n                 parse_dates=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>], chunksize=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>)       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># read only what you need, with the right types</span>\norders.head()                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># first rows</span>\norders.info()                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># columns, dtypes, non-null counts, memory</span>\norders.describe()             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># count, mean, std, min, quartiles, max for numeric columns</span>\norders.shape, orders.dtypes\norders.isna().<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># missing values per column</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>].value_counts(normalize=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># share of each category</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>].nunique()   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 7</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Other readers: <code>read_excel</code>, <code>read_json</code>, <code>read_parquet</code> (fast, typed, compressed: prefer it for big data), <code>read_sql</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-selecting-loc-vs-iloc\">Selecting: <code>loc</code> vs <code>iloc</code></h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">orders.loc[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>:<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>]]     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># LABEL-based, END INCLUSIVE: rows labelled 2, 3 and 4 (3 rows)</span>\norders.iloc[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>:<span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, [<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>]]                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># POSITION-based, end EXCLUSIVE: rows at positions 2 and 3 (2 rows)</span>\norders[orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>] &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>]             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># boolean filter</span>\norders[(orders.amount &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>) &amp; (orders.status == <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;delivered&quot;</span>)]    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># combine with &amp; | ~ and PARENTHESES</span>\norders.query(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount &gt; 1000 and status == &#x27;delivered&#x27;&quot;</span>)            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># readable alternative</span>\norders[orders.user_id.isin([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])]         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># membership, like SQL IN</span>\norders[orders.amount.between(<span class=\"hljs-number\">500</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>)]    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># inclusive by default</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Chained assignment</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p><code>orders[orders.amount &gt; 1000][&quot;status&quot;] = &quot;big&quot;</code> modifies a temporary copy, not <code>orders</code>. Write <code>orders.loc[orders.amount &gt; 1000, &quot;status&quot;] = &quot;big&quot;</code>. Older pandas warns (<code>SettingWithCopyWarning</code>); recent versions use Copy-on-Write, where the chained form silently does nothing. Use <code>.copy()</code> when you want an independent frame.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-creating-and-transforming-columns\">Creating and transforming columns</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">orders = orders.assign(amount_k=<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> d: d.amount / <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>)            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># add a column, chain-friendly</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;month&quot;</span>] = orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>].dt.to_period(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;M&quot;</span>)              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># .dt: date parts (year, month, dayofweek ...)</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;</span>] = pd.cut(orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5000</span>], labels=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;S&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;M&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;L&quot;</span>])     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># fixed bins</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;</span>] = pd.qcut(orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>], <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, labels=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;low&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mid&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;high&quot;</span>])                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># equal-count bins (quantiles)</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;flag&quot;</span>] = np.select([orders.amount &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">2000</span>, orders.amount &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;high&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mid&quot;</span>], default=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;low&quot;</span>)  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># CASE WHEN</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>] = orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>].astype(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;category&quot;</span>)                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># save memory, express intent</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status_upper&quot;</span>] = orders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>].astype(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>.upper()     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># .str accessor: lower, contains, split, replace ...</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><code>map</code> transforms a Series element by element (with a dict or function), <code>apply</code> runs a function along an axis of a DataFrame or over a Series, <code>DataFrame.map</code> (formerly <code>applymap</code>) is element-wise across a frame. Prefer vectorised operations (<code>a + b</code>, <code>np.where</code>, <code>.str</code>, <code>.dt</code>) over all three.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-missing-data-and-duplicates\">Missing data and duplicates</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df = pd.DataFrame({<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;b&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;b&quot;</span>], <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, np.nan, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">50</span>]})\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].fillna(df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].median())                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># simple fill</span>\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].fillna(df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].transform(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;median&quot;</span>))   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># fill with the GROUP&#x27;s median</span>\ndf.dropna(subset=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>])                                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># drop rows missing a value</span>\ndf.drop_duplicates(subset=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept&quot;</span>], keep=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;first&quot;</span>); df.duplicated().<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].clip(lower=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, upper=<span class=\"hljs-number\">40</span>)                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># cap values</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-aggregation-agg-transform-filter\">Aggregation: <code>agg</code>, <code>transform</code>, <code>filter</code></h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).agg(orders=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;nunique&quot;</span>), revenue=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sum&quot;</span>), aov=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mean&quot;</span>)).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># named aggregation; result has one row PER USER:</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#          orders  revenue     aov</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># user_id</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1             4     5100  1275.0</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2             3     3700  1233.3</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ...</span>\n\norders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].transform(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sum&quot;</span>)     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># same length as orders: each row gets ITS user&#x27;s total</span>\norders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">filter</span>(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> g: <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(g) &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># keep whole groups that pass a test (like HAVING)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><code>agg</code> <strong>reduces</strong>; <code>transform</code> <strong>broadcasts back</strong> to the original rows, which is exactly what SQL&#39;s <code>SUM() OVER (PARTITION BY ...)</code> does.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-joining-and-stacking\">Joining and stacking</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">a.merge(b, on=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span>, how=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;left&quot;</span>)                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># SQL join; how = inner | left | right | outer</span>\na.merge(b, left_on=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;uid&quot;</span>, right_on=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, suffixes=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;_a&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;_b&quot;</span>))\na.merge(b, on=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;id&quot;</span>, how=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;outer&quot;</span>, indicator=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;_merge&quot;</span>].value_counts()   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># both / left_only / right_only</span>\na.join(b)                                                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># joins on the INDEX</span>\npd.concat([a, b], axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)                                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># stack rows; axis=1 to place side by side</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-reshaping\">Reshaping</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">orders.pivot_table(index=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, columns=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;</span>, values=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, aggfunc=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;sum&quot;</span>, fill_value=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># like a spreadsheet pivot</span>\norders.melt(id_vars=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>, value_vars=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># wide to long</span>\npd.crosstab(orders.user_id, orders.status)               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># counts table</span>\norders.set_index(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>).sort_index()              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># index on dates enables resample and slicing</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-sequences-and-time\">Sequences and time</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">orders = orders.sort_values([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>])   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ALWAYS sort before shift / diff / rolling</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prev_date&quot;</span>] = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># LAG</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gap_days&quot;</span>] = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>].diff().dt.days  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 15.0, 21.0, 20.0 ... NaN for first order</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cum&quot;</span>] = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].cumsum()\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;n&quot;</span>] = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).cumcount() + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ROW_NUMBER per group</span>\norders.set_index(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].resample(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;MS&quot;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># month-start totals</span>\norders[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].rolling(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, min_periods=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).mean()         <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># rolling average; expanding() grows; ewm() weights recent</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-performance-habits\">Performance habits</h3>\n<p>Use the right dtypes (<code>category</code>, <code>int32</code>, <code>float32</code>), read only needed columns, read big files in <code>chunksize</code> pieces, avoid <code>iterrows</code> and row-wise <code>apply</code>, store intermediate data as <strong>Parquet</strong>, and know that <strong>Polars</strong> and <strong>DuckDB</strong> (SQL on DataFrames and files) exist for bigger-than-memory or faster workloads.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-checklist~1","q":"How many rows does <code>orders.loc[2:4]</code> return versus <code>orders.iloc[2:4]</code> on a default index?","a":"<p><code>loc</code> slices by label and includes both ends, so labels 2, 3 and 4 give <strong>3 rows</strong>. <code>iloc</code> slices by position like Python lists and excludes the end, so positions 2 and 3 give <strong>2 rows</strong>. This is a favourite trick question; with a non-default index the two can select completely different rows.</p>\n","s":"3 rows versus 2: <code>loc</code> includes the end label, <code>iloc</code> excludes the end position."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-checklist~2","q":"After <code>df = pd.DataFrame({&quot;a&quot;: [0, 2, 3], &quot;b&quot;: [1, 1, 1]})</code>, what does <code>df.loc[df.a &gt; 1, &quot;b&quot;] = 0</code> produce, and why is <code>df[df.a &gt; 1][&quot;b&quot;] = 0</code> wrong?","a":"<p>The <code>.loc</code> form performs one assignment on the original frame, giving <code>b = [1, 0, 0]</code>. The chained form first evaluates <code>df[df.a &gt; 1]</code> (which may return a copy), then assigns into that temporary object, so <code>df</code> is not updated (and pandas warns or, with Copy-on-Write, does nothing). Always combine the row selector and column selector in a single <code>.loc</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>b</code> becomes <code>[1, 0, 0]</code>; the chained version edits a copy."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-checklist~ca0_1","q":"How do you add each row&#39;s department average salary as a new column?","a":"<p><code>df[&#39;dept_avg&#39;] = df.groupby(&#39;dept&#39;)[&#39;salary&#39;].transform(&#39;mean&#39;)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-checklist~ca0_2","q":"Why write <code>df.loc[mask, &#39;col&#39;] = 0</code> instead of <code>df[mask][&#39;col&#39;] = 0</code>?","a":"<p>The chained version may modify a temporary copy and warn; loc assigns to the original in one step.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-checklist~ca0_3","q":"What does <code>merge</code> do by default, and what do you check afterwards?","a":"<p>An inner join on common columns. Check row counts and use <code>validate=&#39;one_to_one&#39;</code> or similar to catch duplicate-key fan-out.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-pandas-checklist~ta1_1","q":"<p>Using the same five orders, write one line that adds a column <code>is_biggest</code> that is True for each user&#39;s largest order, and give its values.</p>\n","a":"<p><code>df[&quot;is_biggest&quot;] = df[&quot;amount&quot;] == df.groupby(&quot;user&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].transform(&quot;max&quot;)</code>. The group maxima per row are 300, 150, 300, 80, 150, so the values are False, False, True, True, True. User c&#39;s only order is its largest. With a tie, both tied rows would be True.</p>\n"}]},"m3-sql-to-pandas":{"id":"m3-sql-to-pandas","slug":"sql-to-pandas-translation-table","m":3,"title":"SQL to pandas translation table","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m3-pandas-checklist","m2-level-3"],"tags":["pandas","sql"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A side-by-side translation of SQL concepts (select, where, group by, joins, window functions, CASE WHEN, HAVING) into pandas, with verified outputs.","keyTerms":["SQL to pandas","window functions in pandas","transform","cumcount","shift","np.select"],"interviewQs":["Translate ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY v DESC) to pandas.","How do you express HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 in pandas?","What is the pandas equivalent of SUM(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g)?"],"mistakes":["Forgetting to sort before cumcount or shift","Using apply with a lambda where transform is enough","Filtering with and/or instead of & and |"],"practice":"Take five SQL queries from Module 2 and translate each to pandas, then compare the outputs on the same data.","related":["m3-pandas-checklist","m2-level-3","m3-pandas-problems"],"points":"SQL - pandas\nSELECT col1, col2 - df[['col1','col2']]\nWHERE a > 5 AND b = 'x' - df[(df.a > 5) & (df.b == 'x')]\nGROUP BY k + SUM(v) - df.groupby('k')['v'].sum()\nCOUNT(DISTINCT u) - df['u'].nunique()\nORDER BY v DESC LIMIT 5 - df.nlargest(5, 'v') or sort_values('v', ascending=False).head(5)\nLEFT JOIN - a.merge(b, on='k', how='left')\nROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY v DESC) - df['rn'] = df.sort_values('v', ascending=False).groupby('g').cumcount() + 1\nLAG(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY t) - df.sort_values('t').groupby('g')['v'].shift(1)\nSUM(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g) - df.groupby('g')['v'].transform('sum')\nCASE WHEN - np.select([cond1, cond2], [v1, v2], default=v3)\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 1 - df.groupby('k').filter(lambda g: len(g) > 1)"},"intuitive":"<p>If you already know SQL, pandas is mostly a change of spelling. &quot;WHERE&quot; becomes a yes/no filter on the table, &quot;GROUP BY&quot; becomes groupby, &quot;JOIN&quot; becomes merge, and window functions (the tricky part) become groupby followed by transform, shift, cumsum or cumcount. The big idea: SQL keeps every row when you use a window function; pandas does the same with transform.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers often give a SQL-style task in pandas, or ask you to rewrite one in the other.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Learn the twelve pairs in the table and you can move between SQL and pandas in either direction.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Most pandas interview questions are SQL ideas in different syntax. WHERE becomes a boolean mask. SELECT becomes a column list. GROUP BY with an aggregate becomes groupby then agg. JOIN becomes merge with how equal to inner or left. ORDER BY and LIMIT become sort_values and head. A window function like ROW_NUMBER over partition becomes groupby then cumcount or rank, and LAG becomes groupby then shift. COUNT DISTINCT becomes nunique. HAVING becomes filtering the grouped result. So I translate the SQL in my head first. The catch: pandas count skips NaN like SQL, and a left merge keeps unmatched rows with NaN, so I check for missing after merging.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Translate a query clause by clause","html":"<p>SQL from topic 2.2: users with at least two delivered orders before March, by total spend.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>SQL</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">SELECT</span> user_id, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> n, <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">SUM</span>(amount) <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AS</span> spend\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">FROM</span> orders\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">WHERE</span> status <span class=\"hljs-operator\">=</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;delivered&#x27;</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">AND</span> order_date <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&lt;</span> <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;2025-03-01&#x27;</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">GROUP</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> user_id <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">HAVING</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">COUNT</span>(<span class=\"hljs-operator\">*</span>) <span class=\"hljs-operator\">&gt;=</span> <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">ORDER</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">BY</span> spend <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">DESC</span>;</code></pre></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>FROM:</strong> the DataFrame <code>orders</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>WHERE becomes a boolean mask</strong> (note the brackets and <code>&amp;</code>):\n<code>d = orders[(orders.status == &quot;delivered&quot;) &amp; (orders.order_date &lt; &quot;2025-03-01&quot;)]</code></li>\n<li><strong>GROUP BY plus the aggregates becomes groupby plus named agg:</strong>\n<code>g = d.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;).agg(n=(&quot;amount&quot;, &quot;size&quot;), spend=(&quot;amount&quot;, &quot;sum&quot;))</code></li>\n<li><strong>HAVING becomes a filter on the grouped result:</strong> <code>g = g[g.n &gt;= 2]</code></li>\n<li><strong>ORDER BY becomes sort_values:</strong> <code>g.sort_values(&quot;spend&quot;, ascending=False)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Result:</strong> user 2 (n 2, spend 3,400), then user 1 (n 3, spend 2,900), exactly as in SQL.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>SQL</th><th>pandas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WHERE</td><td>mask <code>df[(cond1) &amp; (cond2)]</code></td></tr><tr><td>GROUP BY ... COUNT, SUM</td><td><code>groupby().agg(name=(col, func))</code></td></tr><tr><td>HAVING</td><td>filter the grouped frame</td></tr><tr><td>ORDER BY ... DESC</td><td><code>sort_values(..., ascending=False)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Both languages run the same pipeline: filter rows, group, aggregate, filter groups, sort. Translate one clause at a time.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-translation-table-verified","text":"The translation table, verified","level":3},{"id":"h-same-answers-as-the-sql-module","text":"Same answers as the SQL module","level":3},{"id":"h-where-the-two-differ","text":"Where the two differ","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>SQL</th><th>pandas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SELECT col1, col2</code></td><td><code>df[[&#39;col1&#39;,&#39;col2&#39;]]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE a &gt; 5 AND b = &#39;x&#39;</code></td><td><code>df[(df.a &gt; 5) &amp; (df.b == &#39;x&#39;)]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>GROUP BY k</code> + <code>SUM(v)</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&#39;k&#39;)[&#39;v&#39;].sum()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT u)</code></td><td><code>df[&#39;u&#39;].nunique()</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORDER BY v DESC LIMIT 5</code></td><td><code>df.nlargest(5, &#39;v&#39;)</code> or <code>sort_values(&#39;v&#39;, ascending=False).head(5)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LEFT JOIN</code></td><td><code>a.merge(b, on=&#39;k&#39;, how=&#39;left&#39;)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY v DESC)</code></td><td><code>df[&#39;rn&#39;] = df.sort_values(&#39;v&#39;, ascending=False).groupby(&#39;g&#39;).cumcount() + 1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LAG(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY t)</code></td><td><code>df.sort_values(&#39;t&#39;).groupby(&#39;g&#39;)[&#39;v&#39;].shift(1)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>SUM(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g)</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&#39;g&#39;)[&#39;v&#39;].transform(&#39;sum&#39;)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>CASE WHEN</code></td><td><code>np.select([cond1, cond2], [v1, v2], default=v3)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&#39;k&#39;).filter(lambda g: len(g) &gt; 1)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-translation-table-verified\">The translation table, verified</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>SQL</th><th>pandas</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SELECT col1, col2</code></td><td><code>df[[&quot;col1&quot;, &quot;col2&quot;]]</code></td><td>double brackets return a DataFrame</td></tr><tr><td><code>WHERE a &gt; 5 AND b = &#39;x&#39;</code></td><td><code>df[(df.a &gt; 5) &amp; (df.b == &quot;x&quot;)]</code></td><td>use <code>&amp;</code>, <code>|</code>, <code>~</code> with parentheses</td></tr><tr><td><code>GROUP BY k</code> + <code>SUM(v)</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&quot;k&quot;)[&quot;v&quot;].sum()</code></td><td>add <code>.reset_index()</code> for a flat frame</td></tr><tr><td><code>COUNT(DISTINCT u)</code></td><td><code>df[&quot;u&quot;].nunique()</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>ORDER BY v DESC LIMIT 5</code></td><td><code>df.nlargest(5, &quot;v&quot;)</code></td><td>or <code>sort_values(&quot;v&quot;, ascending=False).head(5)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>LEFT JOIN</code></td><td><code>a.merge(b, on=&quot;k&quot;, how=&quot;left&quot;)</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY v DESC)</code></td><td><code>df.sort_values(&quot;v&quot;, ascending=False).groupby(&quot;g&quot;).cumcount() + 1</code></td><td>sort first</td></tr><tr><td><code>LAG(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g ORDER BY t)</code></td><td><code>df.sort_values(&quot;t&quot;).groupby(&quot;g&quot;)[&quot;v&quot;].shift(1)</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>SUM(v) OVER (PARTITION BY g)</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&quot;g&quot;)[&quot;v&quot;].transform(&quot;sum&quot;)</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>CASE WHEN</code></td><td><code>np.select([c1, c2], [v1, v2], default=v3)</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&quot;k&quot;).filter(lambda g: len(g) &gt; 1)</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>RANK() / DENSE_RANK()</code></td><td><code>df.groupby(&quot;g&quot;)[&quot;v&quot;].rank(method=&quot;min&quot; / &quot;dense&quot;, ascending=False)</code></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-same-answers-as-the-sql-module\">Same answers as the SQL module</h3>\n<p>Using the <code>employees</code> data (Ravi and Meera tie on 70,000):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">emp = pd.DataFrame({<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Asha&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Ravi&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Meera&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Karan&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Isha&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Dev&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Nina&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Omar&quot;</span>,<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Zoya&quot;</span>],\n                    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept_id&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>],\n                    <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>: [<span class=\"hljs-number\">90000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">70000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">70000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">50000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">80000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">60000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">60000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">55000</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">95000</span>]})\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY dept_id ORDER BY salary DESC)</span>\nemp[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dr&quot;</span>] = emp.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].rank(method=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dense&quot;</span>, ascending=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>).astype(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">int</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Top 3 per department, ties included (matches SQL problem P6): 9 rows</span>\ntop3 = emp[emp[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dr&quot;</span>] &lt;= <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>]\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># ROW_NUMBER: sort, then number within each group</span>\nemp[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rn&quot;</span>] = emp.sort_values(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>, ascending=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>).groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dept_id&quot;</span>).cumcount() + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Note: rn aligns back on the index, so the result is correct even though we sorted a copy.</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Running total, previous value and percent growth on the orders (SQL problems P9 to P12):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">daily = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>, as_index=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\ndaily[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;running_total&quot;</span>] = daily[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].cumsum()                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># SUM(SUM(amount)) OVER (ORDER BY date)</span>\n\nmonthly = orders.set_index(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>).resample(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;MS&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>().to_frame(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>)\nmonthly[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mom_pct&quot;</span>] = monthly[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rev&quot;</span>].pct_change() * <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>               <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># LAG + growth</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\">#              rev  mom_pct</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2025-01-01  8200      NaN</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2025-02-01  5200   -36.59</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2025-03-01  8100    55.77</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>The numbers match the SQL results in Module 2 (−36.59 and 55.77), which is the point: same logic, different syntax.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-where-the-two-differ\">Where the two differ</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Missing values</strong>: pandas skips <code>NaN</code> in most reductions by default (<code>skipna=True</code>), like SQL ignoring NULL; but <code>NaN != NaN</code>, and <code>df.a == np.nan</code> is always False, so use <code>isna()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Grouping keys with NaN</strong>: <code>groupby</code> drops NaN keys by default (<code>dropna=True</code>), unlike SQL which groups NULLs together. Pass <code>dropna=False</code> to keep them.</li>\n<li><strong>Integer division</strong> is not a trap in pandas (<code>/</code> is true division).</li>\n<li><strong>Order</strong>: a DataFrame has a row order (the index); SQL tables do not. Always sort explicitly before <code>shift</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>cumsum</code> and <code>cumcount</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-sql-to-pandas~1","q":"Translate to pandas: users with more than 2 orders, showing the count.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">counts = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).size()          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># COUNT(*) per user</span>\ncounts[counts &gt; <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]                                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 2</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># user_id</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1    4</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2    3</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>or keep the original rows of those users: <code>orders.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;).filter(lambda g: len(g) &gt; 2)</code>. The first returns one row per qualifying user; the second returns all their order rows.</p>\n","s":"<code>orders.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;).size().loc[lambda s: s &gt; 2]</code>"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-sql-to-pandas~ca0_1","q":"Translate <code>SELECT dept, AVG(salary) FROM emp GROUP BY dept HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 5</code>.","a":"<p><code>g = emp.groupby(&#39;dept&#39;).agg(avg=(&#39;salary&#39;,&#39;mean&#39;), n=(&#39;salary&#39;,&#39;size&#39;)); g[g.n &gt; 5][&#39;avg&#39;]</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-sql-to-pandas~ca0_2","q":"Pandas equivalent of <code>LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user ORDER BY date)</code>?","a":"<p>Sort by date, then <code>df.groupby(&#39;user&#39;)[&#39;amount&#39;].shift(1)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-sql-to-pandas~ca0_3","q":"Users with no orders: SQL anti-join in pandas?","a":"<p><code>merge(..., how=&#39;left&#39;, indicator=True)</code> then keep <code>_merge == &#39;left_only&#39;</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-sql-to-pandas~ta1_1","q":"<p>Translate to pandas: <code>SELECT user_id, order_date, amount - LAG(amount) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY order_date) AS change FROM orders</code>.</p>\n","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">o = orders.sort_values([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>])\no[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;change&quot;</span>] = o[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>] - o.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].shift(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>sort_values</code> provides the ORDER BY inside each partition; <code>groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;)</code> is the PARTITION BY; <code>shift(1)</code> is LAG. The first order of each user gets NaN, just as LAG gives NULL.</p>\n"}]},"m3-pandas-problems":{"id":"m3-pandas-problems","slug":"worked-pandas-problems","m":3,"title":"Worked pandas problems","min":40,"type":"practice","priority":"High","prereq":["m3-sql-to-pandas"],"tags":["pandas","practice"],"qa":17,"ai":{"scope":"Ten worked pandas problems (top N per group, growth, rolling, imputation, retention matrix, outliers, explode, merge diagnostics) with explanations and extra variations.","keyTerms":["groupby head","resample","rolling","transform median","named aggregation","cohort retention","IQR outliers","explode","merge indicator"],"interviewQs":["Top 3 products by revenue within each category in pandas.","Build a cohort retention matrix in pandas.","Detect outliers with the IQR rule."],"mistakes":["Forgetting as_index=False, leaving group keys in the index","Using apply with lambda where transform works","Rolling over unsorted data"],"practice":"Re-solve each problem on your own data, then vary the requirement (top 2, 14-day window, median instead of mean).","related":["m3-sql-to-pandas","m3-pandas-checklist"],"points":"Problem: pandas: Top 3 products by revenue within each category\n\nProblem: pandas: Month-over-month growth\n\nProblem: pandas: 7-day rolling average per user\n\nProblem: pandas: Fill missing values with group median\n\nProblem: pandas: Named aggregation\n\nProblem: pandas: Days since last order per user\n\nProblem: pandas: Retention matrix\n\nProblem: pandas: Outliers via IQR\n\nProblem: pandas: Explode a list column / split strings\n\nProblem: pandas: Merge diagnostics"},"intuitive":"<p>These ten problems are the &quot;greatest hits&quot; of pandas interviews: find the best items per group, see month-on-month growth, smooth a noisy series, fill missing values sensibly, compute retention, catch outliers, unpack lists stored in one cell, and check whether two tables really match. Each one is a small recipe: group, aggregate, sort, and shape the answer.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> They test whether you can turn a business question into two or three chained operations.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Think in steps: group, compute, sort, reshape; verify with a tiny example.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;For a pandas problem I do three things. First I state the output shape: one row per user, per day, or per group. Second I pick the tool: groupby for aggregation, transform for a per-row group value, merge to combine, pivot_table to reshape, rank for top N. Third I build in small steps and print after each one. The classic ones are top N per group using sort then groupby head, de-duplicating while keeping the latest row with sort then drop_duplicates, a rolling average with rolling, and a streak using the cumsum-on-change trick. The catch: vectorised code beats apply with a Python function, and I must handle NaN and ties explicitly.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Group consecutive runs with \"compare to the previous row\"","html":"<p>A sensor reports a status every minute: A, A, B, B, B, A. Label each run of identical values (the pandas cousin of SQL&#39;s gaps and islands).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Compare each value with the previous one:</strong> <code>s != s.shift()</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>minute</th><th>status</th><th>previous</th><th>changed?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>A</td><td>NaN</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A</td><td>A</td><td>False</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>B</td><td>A</td><td>True</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>False</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>B</td><td>B</td><td>False</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>A</td><td>B</td><td>True</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>A running count of the changes is the run id:</strong> <code>(s != s.shift()).cumsum()</code> gives 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Group by the run id</strong> to get each run&#39;s length and status: run 1 = A × 2, run 2 = B × 3, run 3 = A × 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Answer the question.</strong> &quot;Longest time in state B&quot;: 3 minutes (run 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Per entity,</strong> add the entity to the comparison, for example <code>(df.status != df.groupby(&quot;machine&quot;).status.shift()).cumsum()</code>, so a new machine starts a new run.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> &quot;Flag where something changes, then take a running sum&quot; turns a row-by-row problem into a single vectorised line.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-to-read-each-solution","text":"How to read each solution","level":3},{"id":"h-verified-outputs-to-compare-against","text":"Verified outputs to compare against","level":3},{"id":"h-more-problems-with-solutions","text":"More problems, with solutions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#1","src":"c","q":"pandas: Top 3 products by revenue within each category","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">(df.groupby([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;product&#x27;</span>], as_index=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;revenue&#x27;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\n   .sort_values([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;revenue&#x27;</span>], ascending=[<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, <span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>])\n   .groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;category&#x27;</span>).head(<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>))</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Sum revenue per product, sort by category then revenue descending, and take <code>groupby(&quot;category&quot;).head(3)</code>.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#2","src":"c","q":"pandas: Month-over-month growth","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">m = (df.set_index(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_date&#x27;</span>)\n       .resample(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;MS&#x27;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;amount&#x27;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\n       .to_frame(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rev&#x27;</span>))\nm[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;mom_pct&#x27;</span>] = m[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rev&#x27;</span>].pct_change() * <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span></code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>resample(&quot;MS&quot;).sum()</code> then <code>pct_change() * 100</code>.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#3","src":"c","q":"pandas: 7-day rolling average per user","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df = df.sort_values([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user_id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;date&#x27;</span>])\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;ma7&#x27;</span>] = df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user_id&#x27;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;value&#x27;</span>].transform(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> s: s.rolling(<span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>, min_periods=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).mean())</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Sort by user and date, then group and apply a rolling mean with <code>transform</code>.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#4","src":"c","q":"pandas: Fill missing values with group median","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;salary&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;salary&#x27;</span>].fillna(df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;dept&#x27;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;salary&#x27;</span>].transform(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;median&#x27;</span>))</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>fillna(groupby(&quot;dept&quot;)[&quot;salary&quot;].transform(&quot;median&quot;))</code>.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#5","src":"c","q":"pandas: Named aggregation","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;city&#x27;</span>).agg(orders=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_id&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;nunique&#x27;</span>),\n                       revenue=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;amount&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;sum&#x27;</span>),\n                       aov=(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;amount&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;mean&#x27;</span>))</code></pre></div>\n","s":"<code>groupby(&quot;city&quot;).agg(orders=(&quot;order_id&quot;, &quot;nunique&quot;), ...)</code>: named aggregation.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#6","src":"c","q":"pandas: Days since last order per user","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> pandas <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> pd, numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n\nlast = df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user_id&#x27;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_date&#x27;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">max</span>()\nrecency = (pd.Timestamp.today() - last).dt.days</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Latest order per user, then today&#39;s (or an as-of) date minus that, in days.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#7","src":"c","q":"pandas: Retention matrix","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cohort&#x27;</span>] = df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user_id&#x27;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_date&#x27;</span>].transform(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;min&#x27;</span>).dt.to_period(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;M&#x27;</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;period&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;order_date&#x27;</span>].dt.to_period(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;M&#x27;</span>)\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;n&#x27;</span>] = (df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;period&#x27;</span>] - df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cohort&#x27;</span>]).apply(<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda</span> x: x.n)\ncohort = df.groupby([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;cohort&#x27;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;n&#x27;</span>])[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;user_id&#x27;</span>].nunique().unstack(fill_value=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nretention = cohort.div(cohort[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>], axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Cohort month, months since cohort, distinct users per cell, unstack, divide each row by month 0.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#8","src":"c","q":"pandas: Outliers via IQR","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">q1, q3 = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;x&#x27;</span>].quantile([<span class=\"hljs-number\">.25</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">.75</span>]); iqr = q3 - q1\noutliers = df[(df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;x&#x27;</span>] &lt; q1 - <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.5</span> * iqr) | (df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;x&#x27;</span>] &gt; q3 + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1.5</span> * iqr)]</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Flag values below Q1 − 1.5·IQR or above Q3 + 1.5·IQR.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#9","src":"c","q":"pandas: Explode a list column / split strings","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;tag&#x27;</span>] = df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;tags&#x27;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>.split(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;,&#x27;</span>)\ndf = df.explode(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;tag&#x27;</span>)</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Split the string into a list, then <code>explode</code> to one row per element.","code":1},{"k":"qa","id":"3.4#10","src":"c","q":"pandas: Merge diagnostics","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">out = a.merge(b, on=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;id&#x27;</span>, how=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;outer&#x27;</span>, indicator=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\nout[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;_merge&#x27;</span>].value_counts()</code></pre></div>\n","s":"Outer merge with <code>indicator=True</code> and <code>value_counts</code> the <code>_merge</code> column.","code":1},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-to-read-each-solution\">How to read each solution</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Problem 1, top 3 per group.</strong> Aggregate to product level first, sort by <code>[category, revenue]</code> (category ascending, revenue descending), then <code>groupby(&quot;category&quot;).head(3)</code>. Sorting first is what makes <code>head</code> mean &quot;top&quot;. Alternative: <code>rank(method=&quot;first&quot;)</code> per group then filter.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 2, month-over-month.</strong> <code>resample(&quot;MS&quot;)</code> buckets dates by month start; <code>pct_change()</code> compares with the previous row. Empty months appear as 0 after <code>.sum()</code> (important: a month with no orders is 0 revenue, not missing).</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 3, rolling average per user.</strong> Sort by user and date, then <code>groupby(...).transform(lambda s: s.rolling(7, min_periods=1).mean())</code>. <code>min_periods=1</code> avoids NaN for the first 6 rows. Rolling windows count <strong>rows</strong> unless you use a time-based window (<code>rolling(&quot;7D&quot;)</code> on a datetime index).</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 4, fill with the group median.</strong> <code>transform(&quot;median&quot;)</code> returns a Series aligned to the original rows, so <code>fillna</code> fits perfectly.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 5, named aggregation.</strong> <code>agg(name=(column, function))</code> produces clean column names in one step.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 6, days since last order.</strong> Reduce to one date per user with <code>max()</code>, subtract from a reference date, take <code>.dt.days</code>. Use a <strong>fixed as-of date</strong> in analysis for reproducibility, not <code>Timestamp.today()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 7, retention matrix.</strong> Cohort = user&#39;s first month; period = activity month; <code>n</code> = months since cohort; count distinct users per <code>(cohort, n)</code>, unstack into a matrix, divide each row by its <code>n = 0</code> column.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 8, IQR outliers.</strong> <code>Q1 - 1.5*IQR</code> and <code>Q3 + 1.5*IQR</code> are the standard &quot;whisker&quot; fences; values outside are candidate outliers to investigate, not automatically to delete.</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 9, explode.</strong> Split a comma-separated string into a list, then <code>explode</code> so each element gets its own row (the other columns repeat).</li>\n<li><strong>Problem 10, merge diagnostics.</strong> <code>indicator=True</code> adds a <code>_merge</code> column telling you which rows exist in both, left only or right only: the fastest check for join mismatches.</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-verified-outputs-to-compare-against\">Verified outputs to compare against</h3>\n<p>Retention matrix from the sample orders (rows are cohorts, columns are months since first order):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>cohort</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2025-01</td><td>4</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>2025-02</td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>2025-03</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Divided by the first column: January keeps 100%, 75%, 75%; February 100%, 100% (and month 2 is not observed yet, shown as 0). The zeros in unobserved cells are an artefact of <code>fill_value=0</code>; say so, or mask them.</p>\n<p>IQR on <code>[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 100]</code>: Q1 = 2.75, Q3 = 6.25, IQR = 3.5, fences = −2.5 and 11.5, so only <code>100</code> is flagged.</p>\n<p><code>merge(..., indicator=True)</code> on ids <code>{1,2,3}</code> and <code>{2,3,4}</code> gives <code>both: 2</code>, <code>left_only: 1</code>, <code>right_only: 1</code>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-problems-with-solutions\">More problems, with solutions</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-problems~1","q":"pandas: for each user, keep only their latest order.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">latest = (orders.sort_values([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>])\n                .groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>).tail(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>))\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># equivalent:</span>\nlatest = orders.sort_values(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>).drop_duplicates(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, keep=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;last&quot;</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># equivalent with idxmax:</span>\nlatest = orders.loc[orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>].idxmax()]</code></pre></div>\n<p>All three give one row per user (the SQL <code>ROW_NUMBER() ... DESC</code> = 1 pattern). <code>idxmax</code> fails if a group has only NaN dates.</p>\n","s":"Sort by date and take <code>groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;).tail(1)</code> (or <code>drop_duplicates(keep=&quot;last&quot;)</code>)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-problems~2","q":"pandas: share of each user&#39;s spend in the total, as a percentage.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">spend = orders.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()\nshare = (spend / spend.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>() * <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).sort_values(ascending=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># user_id 4: 26.0, 1: 23.7, 2: 17.2 ...</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Dividing a Series by a scalar total is the pandas version of SQL&#39;s <code>SUM(x) / SUM(SUM(x)) OVER ()</code>.</p>\n","s":"<code>spend = groupby.sum()</code>, then <code>spend / spend.sum() * 100</code>."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-problems~3","q":"pandas: users who ordered in January but not in February.","a":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">jan = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">set</span>(orders.loc[orders.order_date.between(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-01-31&quot;</span>), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>])\nfeb = <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">set</span>(orders.loc[orders.order_date.between(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-01&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;2025-02-28&quot;</span>), <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>])\njan - feb            <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># {4}</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>With sets the logic is obvious; for a large frame use <code>Series.isin</code> and boolean masks instead. Note <code>between</code> is inclusive, so for timestamps prefer <code>(d &gt;= start) &amp; (d &lt; next_start)</code>.</p>\n","s":"Build two sets of user ids and subtract them."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-problems~ca0_1","q":"Top 2 salaries per department in pandas?","a":"<p><code>df.sort_values(&#39;salary&#39;, ascending=False).groupby(&#39;dept&#39;).head(2)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-problems~ca0_2","q":"Keep only each user&#39;s latest row.","a":"<p><code>df.sort_values(&#39;ts&#39;).drop_duplicates(&#39;user&#39;, keep=&#39;last&#39;)</code>.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-problems~ca0_3","q":"Why avoid <code>df.apply(f, axis=1)</code> on a million rows?","a":"<p>It loops in Python row by row; vectorised column operations run in compiled code and are orders of magnitude faster.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-pandas-problems~ta1_1","q":"<p><code>df</code> has columns <code>user</code>, <code>ts</code> and <code>amount</code>, one row per order. Write the pandas for &quot;the latest order of each user&quot; and say why the order of the operations matters.</p>\n","a":"<p><code>df.sort_values(&quot;ts&quot;).drop_duplicates(&quot;user&quot;, keep=&quot;last&quot;)</code>. Sorting by time first puts each user&#39;s latest order last, so <code>keep=&quot;last&quot;</code> keeps it. Without the sort, &quot;last&quot; just means &quot;last in the file&quot;, which is arbitrary. An alternative that keeps ties is <code>df[df.ts == df.groupby(&quot;user&quot;).ts.transform(&quot;max&quot;)]</code>.</p>\n"}]},"m3-pandas-concepts":{"id":"m3-pandas-concepts","slug":"conceptual-pandas-questions","m":3,"title":"Conceptual pandas questions","min":15,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m3-pandas-checklist"],"tags":["pandas","interview-questions"],"qa":13,"ai":{"scope":"Conceptual pandas questions: indexing, element-wise methods, joining, transform vs agg, big files, slowness of iterrows and copy semantics.","keyTerms":["loc vs iloc","apply vs map","merge vs join vs concat","transform vs agg","pivot vs pivot_table","chunksize","iterrows","view vs copy"],"interviewQs":["How would you handle a 10 GB CSV?","Why is iterrows slow?","transform vs agg?"],"mistakes":["Reading a whole huge CSV into memory","Using apply for arithmetic that vectorises","Mixing up join and merge"],"practice":"Explain each row in 30 seconds with a code example.","related":["m3-pandas-checklist","m3-pandas-problems"],"points":"Q: loc vs iloc?\nA: loc label-based and inclusive of end label; iloc integer-position-based, end exclusive.\n\nQ: apply vs map vs applymap?\nA: map element-wise on Series; apply along axis of DataFrame or on Series; applymap (now DataFrame.map) element-wise on DataFrame. Vectorized ops beat all three.\n\nQ: merge vs join vs concat?\nA: merge: SQL-style join on columns; join: join on index; concat: stack along axis.\n\nQ: transform vs agg?\nA: agg reduces to one row per group; transform returns same-length result aligned to original rows.\n\nQ: pivot vs pivot_table?\nA: pivot needs unique index/column pairs; pivot_table aggregates duplicates.\n\nQ: How to handle a 10 GB CSV?\nA: chunksize, usecols, dtype, convert to Parquet, use Dask/Polars/DuckDB/PySpark.\n\nQ: Why is iterrows slow?\nA: Python-level loop creating a Series per row; vectorize or use itertuples.\n\nQ: copy vs view?\nA: Slicing may return a view or a copy; use .copy() explicitly; avoid chained indexing."},"intuitive":"<p>These are the &quot;explain it&quot; questions for pandas. The short version: loc picks by name and iloc by position; merge joins on columns while join works on the row labels; agg squeezes each group to one number while transform keeps every row; and very large files are read in pieces or converted to a faster format so your computer does not run out of memory.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers ask these to check you understand pandas, not only type it.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Know the one-line difference and a tiny example for each.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;The conceptual pandas questions test whether you understand what the library is doing. A Series is one labelled column, a DataFrame a table of Series sharing an index. loc selects by label and includes the end of a slice, iloc by integer position and excludes it. apply runs a Python function, so it is slow; vectorised operations are faster. merge joins on columns, join on the index, concat stacks. A view shares memory with the original and a copy does not, which is the source of the SettingWithCopy warning. Missing values are NaN, and dropna, fillna and isna handle them. The catch: category dtype saves memory for low-cardinality strings, and downcasting numerics helps big data.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"loc and iloc, with an index that is not 0, 1, 2","html":"<p><code>d = pd.DataFrame({&quot;x&quot;: [1, 2, 3, 4]}, index=[10, 20, 30, 40])</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><code>d.loc[10:30]</code></strong> selects by label, and label slices <em>include</em> the end: labels 10, 20, 30, so x = 1, 2, 3 (three rows).</li>\n<li><strong><code>d.iloc[0:2]</code></strong> selects by position, and position slices <em>exclude</em> the end: positions 0 and 1, so x = 1, 2 (two rows).</li>\n<li><strong>Now shuffle the index:</strong> <code>d2 = pd.DataFrame({&quot;x&quot;: [1, 2, 3]}, index=[2, 0, 1])</code>.</li>\n<li><strong><code>d2.loc[0]</code></strong> finds the row whose <em>label</em> is 0: x = 2.</li>\n<li><strong><code>d2.iloc[0]</code></strong> takes the <em>first</em> row: x = 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Why it matters.</strong> After filtering or sorting, labels and positions stop matching. Code that mixes them up gives wrong rows with no error.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> <code>loc</code> asks &quot;which label?&quot;, <code>iloc</code> asks &quot;which position?&quot;. When in doubt, look at <code>df.index</code> before slicing.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-deeper-answers","text":"Deeper answers","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#1","src":"c","q":"<code>loc</code> vs <code>iloc</code>?","a":"<p><code>loc</code> label-based and inclusive of end label; <code>iloc</code> integer-position-based, end exclusive.</p>\n","s":"<code>loc</code> is label-based with an inclusive end; <code>iloc</code> is position-based with an exclusive end."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#2","src":"c","q":"<code>apply</code> vs <code>map</code> vs <code>applymap</code>?","a":"<p><code>map</code> element-wise on Series; <code>apply</code> along axis of DataFrame or on Series; <code>applymap</code> (now <code>DataFrame.map</code>) element-wise on DataFrame. Vectorized ops beat all three.</p>\n","s":"<code>map</code> is element-wise on a Series; <code>apply</code> works along an axis; <code>DataFrame.map</code> is element-wise on a frame; vectorised ops beat all."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#3","src":"c","q":"<code>merge</code> vs <code>join</code> vs <code>concat</code>?","a":"<p><code>merge</code>: SQL-style join on columns; <code>join</code>: join on index; <code>concat</code>: stack along axis.</p>\n","s":"<code>merge</code> joins on columns like SQL; <code>join</code> joins on the index; <code>concat</code> stacks frames."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#4","src":"c","q":"<code>transform</code> vs <code>agg</code>?","a":"<p><code>agg</code> reduces to one row per group; <code>transform</code> returns same-length result aligned to original rows.</p>\n","s":"<code>agg</code> reduces to one row per group; <code>transform</code> returns a result aligned to every original row."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#5","src":"c","q":"<code>pivot</code> vs <code>pivot_table</code>?","a":"<p><code>pivot</code> needs unique index/column pairs; <code>pivot_table</code> aggregates duplicates.</p>\n","s":"<code>pivot</code> only reshapes and needs unique pairs; <code>pivot_table</code> aggregates duplicates."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#6","src":"c","q":"How to handle a 10 GB CSV?","a":"<p><code>chunksize</code>, <code>usecols</code>, <code>dtype</code>, convert to Parquet, use Dask/Polars/DuckDB/PySpark.</p>\n","s":"Read in chunks with <code>usecols</code> and <code>dtype</code>, convert to Parquet, or use Dask, Polars, DuckDB or PySpark."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#7","src":"c","q":"Why is <code>iterrows</code> slow?","a":"<p>Python-level loop creating a Series per row; vectorize or use <code>itertuples</code>.</p>\n","s":"It builds a Series for every row in a Python loop; vectorise or use <code>itertuples</code>."},{"k":"qa","id":"3.5#8","src":"c","q":"<code>copy</code> vs view?","a":"<p>Slicing may return a view or a copy; use <code>.copy()</code> explicitly; avoid chained indexing.</p>\n","s":"A slice may be a view or a copy; use <code>.copy()</code> explicitly and avoid chained indexing."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-deeper-answers\">Deeper answers</h3>\n<p><strong><code>transform</code> vs <code>agg</code>, seen in shapes.</strong> On the 16-row orders frame grouped by user (7 users): <code>agg(&quot;sum&quot;)</code> returns shape <code>(7,)</code>; <code>transform(&quot;sum&quot;)</code> returns shape <code>(16,)</code>. <code>agg</code> is for summaries; <code>transform</code> for features that need a group statistic on every row (z-score within group, percent of group total, fill with group median).</p>\n<p><strong><code>pivot</code> vs <code>pivot_table</code>.</strong> <code>pivot</code> only reshapes and fails on duplicate index/column pairs; <code>pivot_table</code> aggregates duplicates (mean by default) and accepts <code>fill_value</code>, <code>margins</code>, multiple aggregations.</p>\n<p><strong>A 10 GB CSV on a laptop.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">usecols = [<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>]\ndtypes = {<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>: <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;int32&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>: <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;float32&quot;</span>}\nparts = []\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> chunk <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> pd.read_csv(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;big.csv&quot;</span>, usecols=usecols, dtype=dtypes, parse_dates=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>], chunksize=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1_000_000</span>):\n    parts.append(chunk.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;user_id&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>())       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># reduce each chunk immediately</span>\nresult = pd.concat(parts).groupby(level=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()                  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># combine partial results</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Better: convert once to <strong>Parquet</strong> (<code>df.to_parquet(&quot;big.parquet&quot;)</code>), which is columnar, typed and compressed, then read only the columns you need, or query it with <strong>DuckDB</strong> (<code>duckdb.sql(&quot;SELECT user_id, SUM(amount) FROM &#39;big.parquet&#39; GROUP BY 1&quot;)</code>), <strong>Polars</strong> (lazy, multi-threaded) or <strong>Dask / PySpark</strong> for distributed work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why <code>iterrows</code> is slow.</strong> It creates a new <code>Series</code> object for every row, in a Python-level loop, losing dtypes along the way. Alternatives, fastest first: a vectorised expression; <code>np.where</code>/<code>np.select</code>; <code>df.itertuples()</code> (much faster than <code>iterrows</code>); <code>apply(axis=1)</code> (still a Python loop); a compiled approach (Numba, Polars).</p>\n<p><strong>View vs copy.</strong> Whether a slice is a view or a copy depends on the operation, which is why chained assignment is unreliable. Use <code>.loc</code> for assignment and <code>.copy()</code> when you need independence. Recent pandas versions use <strong>Copy-on-Write</strong>: any derived object behaves as a copy, and data is shared lazily until modified.</p>\n<p><strong><code>merge</code> vs <code>join</code> vs <code>concat</code>.</strong> <code>merge</code> is the SQL-style join on columns (<code>on</code>, <code>left_on</code>, <code>right_on</code>). <code>join</code> joins on the index (shortcut for <code>merge(left_index=True, ...)</code>). <code>concat</code> stacks frames vertically (<code>axis=0</code>, like <code>UNION ALL</code>) or aligns them side by side (<code>axis=1</code>).</p>\n<p><strong><code>apply</code> vs <code>map</code> vs <code>DataFrame.map</code>.</strong> <code>Series.map(f_or_dict)</code> applies element-wise; <code>DataFrame.apply(f, axis=...)</code> applies to whole columns or rows; <code>DataFrame.map(f)</code> (formerly <code>applymap</code>) applies element-wise to the whole frame. All are Python loops under the hood; a vectorised expression beats them.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-pandas-concepts~1","q":"You run <code>df.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;).apply(lambda g: g[&quot;amount&quot;].sum())</code> on 10 million rows and it is slow. What do you change?","a":"<p><code>apply</code> with a lambda calls Python once per group and cannot be optimised. The built-in aggregation (<code>sum</code>, <code>mean</code>, <code>agg</code>) runs in optimised Cython, so <code>df.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].sum()</code> is typically dramatically faster. Reach for <code>apply</code> only when no built-in or vectorised method expresses the logic.</p>\n","s":"Use the built-in <code>df.groupby(&quot;user_id&quot;)[&quot;amount&quot;].sum()</code>: it runs in compiled code."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-concepts~ca0_1","q":"<code>df.loc[0:2]</code> versus <code>df.iloc[0:2]</code>: how many rows?","a":"<p>loc returns 3 rows (label slices include the end); iloc returns 2 (positional, end excluded).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-concepts~ca0_2","q":"Name two ways to cut the memory of a large DataFrame.","a":"<p>Convert low-cardinality strings to <code>category</code> and downcast numeric columns (int64 to int32, float64 to float32); also read only needed columns.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-pandas-concepts~ca0_3","q":"Difference between <code>merge</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>concat</code>?","a":"<p>merge: SQL-style on columns; join: merge on index; concat: stack along an axis without matching keys.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-pandas-concepts~ta1_1","q":"<p>What is wrong with <code>df[df.amount &gt; 100][&quot;flag&quot;] = 1</code>, what might happen, and what is the correct line?</p>\n","a":"<p>It is chained indexing: <code>df[df.amount &gt; 100]</code> may return a <em>copy</em>, and then <code>[&quot;flag&quot;] = 1</code> sets the column on that temporary copy. pandas warns (SettingWithCopyWarning, or under copy-on-write simply leaves <code>df</code> unchanged), and <code>df</code> does not get the flag. Correct: <code>df.loc[df.amount &gt; 100, &quot;flag&quot;] = 1</code>, one operation on the original frame.</p>\n"}]},"m3-viz":{"id":"m3-viz","slug":"visualization-choosing-the-right-chart","m":3,"title":"Visualization: choosing the right chart","min":20,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":[],"tags":["visualization","matplotlib","seaborn"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"Choosing the right chart for the question, reading distributions (histogram, box plot, QQ plot), and the rules of a clear, honest chart, with Matplotlib and Seaborn snippets.","keyTerms":["histogram","box plot","scatter","heatmap","QQ plot","bar chart","line chart","chart choice","skewness"],"interviewQs":["What does a box plot show?","How do you read a QQ plot?","What does a correlation heatmap not tell you?"],"mistakes":["Pie charts with many slices","Truncated bar-chart axes","Dual axes that mislead"],"practice":"Take one dataset and draw the right chart for each row of the chart-choice table, each with a takeaway title.","related":["m3-eda","m5-descriptive"],"points":"Library choices: Matplotlib (control), Seaborn (statistical, quick), Plotly (interactive), Altair (declarative).\n\nGoal - Chart\nDistribution of one numeric variable - Histogram, KDE, box plot, violin\nRelationship between two numerics - Scatter, hexbin, regression plot\nCorrelation among many - Heatmap of correlation matrix, pair plot\nCategory comparison - Bar chart (sorted), lollipop\nTrend over time - Line chart\nPart-to-whole - Stacked bar (prefer over pie)\nCategorical vs numeric - Box/violin per category\nClassification performance - Confusion matrix, ROC, PR curve, calibration plot\n\nGood-chart rules: label axes and units, start bar charts at zero, limit colors, avoid 3D and dual axes, annotate the insight, and always title with the takeaway.\n\nBe able to answer: What does a box plot show? How do you read a QQ plot? What does skewness look like in a histogram? What does a correlation heatmap not tell you (non-linearity, causation)?"},"intuitive":"<p>A chart is a sentence drawn with shapes. Pick the shape that matches the sentence you want to say: &quot;how are these spread out?&quot; is a histogram or box plot; &quot;do these two things move together?&quot; is a scatter plot; &quot;which is biggest?&quot; is a sorted bar chart; &quot;how did it change over time?&quot; is a line. A good chart has a title that says the answer (&quot;Orders fell 10% after the price change&quot;), labelled axes and as little decoration as possible.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Your charts are how non-technical people judge your analysis.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Match the chart to the question, label everything, never mislead, and put the takeaway in the title.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Choose the chart by the question. Comparing categories: bar chart. Change over time: line chart. Distribution of one variable: histogram or box plot. Relationship between two numbers: scatter plot. Many numeric relationships at once: correlation heatmap. Parts of a whole: a stacked bar, rarely a pie. Rules: start bar charts at zero, label axes with units, use one colour unless colour carries meaning, and avoid dual axes that mislead. Always state the takeaway in the title, like &#39;Orders fell 12 per cent after the price change&#39;. The catch: a chart&#39;s job is a decision or insight, so I remove everything that does not support the message.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Pick the chart from the question","html":"<p>One dataset: daily orders for a year, by city and by device. Four questions, four charts.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>&quot;Did orders grow this year?&quot;</strong> Change over time: a <strong>line chart</strong> of weekly orders. A weekly sum smooths out the weekday wobble.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Which city orders the most?&quot;</strong> Comparing categories: a <strong>horizontal bar chart</strong>, sorted from largest to smallest, starting at zero.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Are order sizes skewed?&quot;</strong> Distribution of one variable: a <strong>histogram</strong> of order value, perhaps on a log axis if a few large orders stretch it.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Do bigger baskets come with longer delivery times?&quot;</strong> Relationship between two numbers: a <strong>scatter plot</strong> of basket size against delivery time, with a trend line.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the title as the finding</strong>, not the topic. Not &quot;Orders by week&quot;, but &quot;Orders grew 40% this year, mostly after the July launch&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Remove what does not help:</strong> 3-D effects, a legend when a direct label would do, colours with no meaning.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The question chooses the chart. If you cannot say the question in one sentence, you are not ready to choose.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-reading-the-charts-interviewers-ask-about","text":"Reading the charts interviewers ask about","level":3},{"id":"h-code-you-can-reuse","text":"Code you can reuse","level":3},{"id":"h-the-rules-of-a-good-chart","text":"The rules of a good chart","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Library choices:</strong> Matplotlib (control), Seaborn (statistical, quick), Plotly (interactive), Altair (declarative).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Chart</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Distribution of one numeric variable</td><td>Histogram, KDE, box plot, violin</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship between two numerics</td><td>Scatter, hexbin, regression plot</td></tr><tr><td>Correlation among many</td><td>Heatmap of correlation matrix, pair plot</td></tr><tr><td>Category comparison</td><td>Bar chart (sorted), lollipop</td></tr><tr><td>Trend over time</td><td>Line chart</td></tr><tr><td>Part-to-whole</td><td>Stacked bar (prefer over pie)</td></tr><tr><td>Categorical vs numeric</td><td>Box/violin per category</td></tr><tr><td>Classification performance</td><td>Confusion matrix, ROC, PR curve, calibration plot</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Good-chart rules:</strong> label axes and units, start bar charts at zero, limit colors, avoid 3D and dual axes, annotate the insight, and always title with the takeaway.</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Be able to answer:</strong> What does a box plot show? How do you read a QQ plot? What does skewness look like in a histogram? What does a correlation heatmap not tell you (non-linearity, causation)?</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-reading-the-charts-interviewers-ask-about\">Reading the charts interviewers ask about</h3>\n<p>Explore how shape changes what the plots look like:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"distshape","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Histogram.</strong> Bars show how many values fall in each bin. Look for centre, spread, skew (a long right tail means mean &gt; median), multiple peaks (mixed groups) and outliers. The bin width changes the story: too few bins hide structure, too many look noisy.</li>\n<li><strong>Box plot.</strong> The box spans Q1 to Q3 (the IQR) with a line at the median; whiskers extend to the most extreme points within 1.5 × IQR; dots beyond are flagged outliers. A median off-centre in the box or unequal whiskers signals skew. Use it to compare groups side by side (a box per category).</li>\n<li><strong>Violin plot.</strong> A box plot plus the density shape; good when distributions are bimodal.</li>\n<li><strong>QQ plot.</strong> Plots the data&#39;s quantiles against the quantiles of a normal distribution. Points on the straight line mean &quot;approximately normal&quot;. A curve that bends up at the right end means a heavy right tail; an S-shape means heavy tails on both sides.</li>\n<li><strong>Scatter plot.</strong> Two numeric variables; look for direction, strength, curvature and clusters. Add a regression line cautiously. For huge data use a hexbin or 2D density plot to avoid overplotting.</li>\n<li><strong>Correlation heatmap.</strong> Shows pairwise Pearson correlations. It cannot show non-linear relationships (a perfect parabola gives ~0), says nothing about causation, and is distorted by outliers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-code-you-can-reuse\">Code you can reuse</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> matplotlib.pyplot <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> plt\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> seaborn <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> sns\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, figsize=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3.6</span>))\nsns.histplot(df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>], bins=<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>, kde=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, ax=ax[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]);                 ax[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>].set_title(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Most orders are small; a few are very large&quot;</span>)\nsns.boxplot(data=df, x=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>, y=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>, ax=ax[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]);                    ax[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>].set_title(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Amounts by city&quot;</span>)\nsns.heatmap(df.select_dtypes(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;number&quot;</span>).corr(), annot=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>, fmt=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;.2f&quot;</span>, cmap=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;vlag&quot;</span>, center=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, ax=ax[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>])\nplt.tight_layout()\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Trend over time with the takeaway as the title</span>\nax = df.set_index(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_date&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].resample(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;MS&quot;</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>().plot(marker=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;o&quot;</span>)\nax.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">set</span>(title=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Revenue dipped in February then recovered&quot;</span>, xlabel=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Month&quot;</span>, ylabel=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Revenue (Rs)&quot;</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># QQ plot</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\nstats.probplot(df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>], dist=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;norm&quot;</span>, plot=plt)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Library choice: <strong>Matplotlib</strong> (full control), <strong>Seaborn</strong> (statistical plots in a line), <strong>Plotly</strong> (interactive), <strong>Altair</strong> (declarative grammar).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-rules-of-a-good-chart\">The rules of a good chart</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Label axes and units.</strong> &quot;Revenue (Rs lakh)&quot;, not &quot;value&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Start bar charts at zero.</strong> Bar length encodes value; a truncated axis exaggerates differences (line charts may start elsewhere).</li>\n<li><strong>Limit colours</strong>, use one highlight colour for the point of the chart, and make sure it works for colour-blind readers.</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid 3D, dual y-axes and pie charts with many slices</strong>; they invite misreading.</li>\n<li><strong>Sort categories</strong> by value in bar charts (unless there is a natural order like months).</li>\n<li><strong>Annotate the insight</strong> and title with the takeaway.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-viz~1","q":"A histogram of delivery time shows a long right tail. What does that imply for mean vs median and for what you report?","a":"<p>A long right tail means a few very long deliveries pull the mean above the median. The median better represents the typical delivery; the P90 or P95 captures the bad experiences that customers remember. Report both, and consider a log scale for the plot. The same logic applies to income, order value and session length.</p>\n","s":"Mean &gt; median; report the median (and a high percentile like P90)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-viz~2","q":"Two variables have a correlation of 0. Can you conclude they are unrelated?","a":"<p>Pearson correlation captures <strong>linear</strong> relationships only. If <code>y = x²</code> with x symmetric around zero, the correlation is 0 even though y is fully determined by x. Plot the data, try Spearman correlation for monotonic relationships, and consider mutual information for general dependence.</p>\n","s":"No: Pearson only measures linear association; a parabola can have r = 0."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-viz~ca0_1","q":"Show how revenue split across four regions changed over twelve months. Chart?","a":"<p>A line chart with one line per region, or a stacked area if the total matters; not twelve pie charts.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-viz~ca0_2","q":"Why must bar charts start at zero?","a":"<p>Bar length encodes value; truncating the axis exaggerates small differences and misleads.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-viz~ca0_3","q":"Which chart for a skewed distribution with outliers, compared across 3 groups?","a":"<p>Side-by-side box plots (or violin plots), possibly on a log scale.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-viz~ta1_1","q":"<p>A slide shows two bars: &quot;Old checkout: 95% success&quot; and &quot;New checkout: 97% success&quot;. The y-axis runs from 94% to 98%, so the second bar looks twice as tall. What is wrong, and how would you fix it?</p>\n","a":"<p>A bar&#39;s length represents its value, so the axis must start at zero; truncating it makes a 2-point difference look like a doubling. Either start the axis at 0% (the bars then look nearly equal, which is honest), or, if the 2-point gain is the message, show it directly: a dot plot, or a label &quot;Success rate up from 95% to 97% (+2 points)&quot;, ideally with the confidence interval of the difference.</p>\n"}]},"m3-eda":{"id":"m3-eda","slug":"the-eda-template-and-self-check","m":3,"title":"The EDA template and self-check","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"High","prereq":["m3-pandas-checklist"],"tags":["eda","pandas","workflow"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"A repeatable eight-step EDA workflow with reusable pandas code, what to look for at each step, and how to present findings.","keyTerms":["exploratory data analysis","grain","missingness","MCAR MAR MNAR","outliers","leakage","segment analysis","data quality"],"interviewQs":["Walk me through how you would explore a new dataset.","What is the grain of this table and why does it matter?","How do you decide what to do about missing values?"],"mistakes":["Jumping to modelling before understanding the grain","Plotting everything without a question","Dropping outliers or missing rows without understanding why they exist"],"practice":"Run the eight steps on a new Kaggle dataset in 45 minutes and write five insights with one chart each.","related":["m3-viz","m8-preprocessing","m7-leakage"],"points":"1. Understand the problem and data dictionary; know the grain (one row = what?).\n2. Shape, dtypes, memory, duplicates.\n3. Missingness: how much, which columns, MCAR/MAR/MNAR, patterns.\n4. Univariate: distributions, skew, outliers, cardinality.\n5. Bivariate: feature vs target, feature vs feature (correlation, VIF).\n6. Segment analysis: by time, geography, cohort.\n7. Data quality checks: impossible values, unit mismatches, leakage candidates.\n8. Hypotheses: what to engineer, what to test.\n\n- Translate 5 SQL window queries into pandas without help.\n- Explain broadcasting with an example.\n- Do a full EDA on a new Kaggle dataset in 45 minutes and summarize 5 insights."},"intuitive":"<p>Exploring a dataset is like moving into a new house: before decorating you walk through every room. What is one row exactly? Are any rooms empty (missing values)? Is anything strange (a door that is 10 metres tall, an outlier)? Do the rooms connect the way you expect (joins and relationships)? Only after the walk-through do you decide what to build. Good analysts write down what they found and the questions it raised.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Case rounds and take-home tasks judge your process as much as your result.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Understand the grain, check quality, look at each variable alone and in pairs, segment, and note hypotheses.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;EDA is a short, repeatable routine before modelling. One: shape, dtypes, a few rows. Two: missing values, by column and by pattern. Three: duplicates and impossible values, like negative ages. Four: distributions of each variable, noting skew and outliers. Five: relationships, such as target against each feature and correlations. Six: segment checks, like time trends and group differences. Seven: write down the findings and next steps. For each odd finding I ask whether it is a data error, a real effect or a logging change. The catch: correlation in EDA is a lead for hypotheses, not proof, and I never look at the test set when deciding what to engineer.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A first look at six rows","html":"<p>A sample from a customer file:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>id</th><th>age</th><th>income</th><th>city</th><th>signup</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>34</td><td>52,000</td><td>Pune</td><td>2024-03-01</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>−1</td><td>48,000</td><td>pune</td><td>2024-03-04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>29</td><td></td><td>Delhi</td><td>2024-03-04</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>29</td><td></td><td>Delhi</td><td>2024-03-04</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>41</td><td>9,900,000</td><td>Mumbai</td><td>04/03/2024</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>23</td><td>31,000</td><td>Delhi</td><td>2024-03-09</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Shape and grain.</strong> Is one row one customer? id 3 appears twice with identical values: a <strong>duplicate</strong> row. <code>df.duplicated().sum()</code> = 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Types.</strong> <code>signup</code> mixes two formats (<code>2024-03-01</code> and <code>04/03/2024</code>), so pandas reads it as text. Convert with <code>pd.to_datetime</code> and check what fails.</li>\n<li><strong>Impossible values.</strong> Age −1 is not a real age: probably a placeholder for &quot;unknown&quot;. Treat it as missing, and ask the data owner.</li>\n<li><strong>Missing values.</strong> id 3 has no income. Ask why before filling: did people refuse, or does the form skip the question for some users?</li>\n<li><strong>Outliers.</strong> An income of 9,900,000 may be a typo (an extra zero, or monthly versus yearly) or genuinely high. Check before deciding; do not just delete it.</li>\n<li><strong>Inconsistent categories.</strong> &quot;Pune&quot; and &quot;pune&quot; are the same city. Standardise with <code>.str.strip().str.title()</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Write down what you found</strong> and what you did. That note is the first slide of any analysis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Six rows contained six different problems. EDA is a checklist you run every time, not a set of nice plots.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-3-7","text":"3.7 EDA template (memorize this workflow)","level":2},{"id":"c-3-8","text":"3.8 Self-check (Module 3)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-eight-steps-with-code","text":"The eight steps, with code","level":3},{"id":"h-a-reusable-first-look-function","text":"A reusable first-look function","level":3},{"id":"h-how-to-present-eda-in-an-interview-or-case","text":"How to present EDA in an interview or case","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-3-7\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.7</span> EDA template (memorize this workflow)</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Understand the problem and data dictionary</strong>; know the grain (one row = what?).</li>\n<li><strong>Shape, dtypes, memory, duplicates.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Missingness</strong>: how much, which columns, MCAR/MAR/MNAR, patterns.</li>\n<li><strong>Univariate</strong>: distributions, skew, outliers, cardinality.</li>\n<li><strong>Bivariate</strong>: feature vs target, feature vs feature (correlation, VIF).</li>\n<li><strong>Segment analysis</strong>: by time, geography, cohort.</li>\n<li><strong>Data quality checks</strong>: impossible values, unit mismatches, leakage candidates.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypotheses</strong>: what to engineer, what to test.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-3-8\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.8</span> Self-check (Module 3)</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m3-eda.c1","kind":"self","items":["Translate 5 SQL window queries into pandas without help.","Explain broadcasting with an example.","Do a full EDA on a new Kaggle dataset in 45 minutes and summarize 5 insights."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-eight-steps-with-code\">The eight steps, with code</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Understand the question and the grain.</strong> Write &quot;one row = one ___&quot; before anything else. Mixed-up grain (orders vs order items, users vs sessions) is the most common cause of wrong numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>2. Shape, types, memory, duplicates.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df.shape, df.dtypes, df.memory_usage(deep=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>() / <span class=\"hljs-number\">1e6</span>     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># rows, columns, dtypes, MB</span>\ndf.duplicated().<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>(), df.duplicated(subset=[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;order_id&quot;</span>]).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()  <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># full-row duplicates vs key duplicates</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>3. Missingness.</strong> How much, where, and why?</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df.isna().mean().sort_values(ascending=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>).head(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># share missing per column</span>\ndf[df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;salary&quot;</span>].isna()].describe()                                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># do missing rows differ from the rest?</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Types of missingness: <strong>MCAR</strong> (completely random: safe to drop or mean-impute, rarely true), <strong>MAR</strong> (depends on other observed columns: model-based or group-wise imputation), <strong>MNAR</strong> (depends on the missing value itself, such as high earners not reporting income: add a missing-indicator feature and be careful). Decide with a business explanation, not a default.</p>\n<p><strong>4. Univariate: each variable alone.</strong> Numeric: distribution, skew, outliers. Categorical: cardinality and rare levels.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].describe(percentiles=[<span class=\"hljs-number\">.01</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">.25</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">.75</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">.99</span>])\ndf[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>].value_counts(normalize=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>).head(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>)</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>5. Bivariate: pairs.</strong> Feature versus target and feature versus feature (correlation, VIF for multicollinearity).</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">df.groupby(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;city&quot;</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;amount&quot;</span>].agg([<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;count&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mean&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;median&quot;</span>])\ndf.corr(numeric_only=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>)\npd.crosstab(df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;device&quot;</span>], df[<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;converted&quot;</span>], normalize=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;index&quot;</span>)     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># conversion rate by device</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>6. Segment analysis.</strong> Slice by time, geography, cohort and channel. Averages hide opposite trends in different segments (Simpson&#39;s paradox).</p>\n<p><strong>7. Data quality checks.</strong> Impossible values (negative ages), unit mismatches (seconds vs milliseconds), inconsistent categories (&quot;Delhi&quot;, &quot;delhi&quot;, &quot;New Delhi&quot;), timestamps in the future, and <strong>leakage candidates</strong> (columns created after the outcome, such as <code>refund_amount</code> when predicting cancellation).</p>\n<p><strong>8. Hypotheses and next steps.</strong> What features to engineer, what to test, what to ask the business.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-a-reusable-first-look-function\">A reusable first-look function</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">def</span> <span class=\"hljs-title function_\">first_look</span>(<span class=\"hljs-params\">df, target=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span></span>):\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;<span class=\"hljs-subst\">{df.shape[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]:,}</span> rows x <span class=\"hljs-subst\">{df.shape[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]}</span> cols; <span class=\"hljs-subst\">{df.duplicated().<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>():,}</span> duplicate rows&quot;</span>)\n    out = pd.DataFrame({\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dtype&quot;</span>: df.dtypes.astype(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">str</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing_%&quot;</span>: (df.isna().mean() * <span class=\"hljs-number\">100</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;unique&quot;</span>: df.nunique(),\n        <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;example&quot;</span>: df.iloc[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>],\n    })\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(out.sort_values(<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing_%&quot;</span>, ascending=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(df.describe(include=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;all&quot;</span>).T.head(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20</span>))\n    <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">if</span> target <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">is</span> <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">not</span> <span class=\"hljs-literal\">None</span>:\n        <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(df[target].value_counts(normalize=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>))</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-how-to-present-eda-in-an-interview-or-case\">How to present EDA in an interview or case</h3>\n<p>Lead with the answer, then the evidence: &quot;The drop is concentrated in Android users in Tier-2 cities after version 4.2. Here is the funnel by platform.&quot; Show one chart per message with the takeaway as the title, state assumptions, list what you did not check, and propose the next analysis.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m3-eda~1","q":"You discover 30% of <code>income</code> is missing in a loan dataset. What do you do?","a":"<p>First ask why: did a form field allow skipping it, or do certain applicants (self-employed, high earners) tend to omit it? Check whether missing rows differ in other columns and in the target (default rate). If it looks random, simple median imputation inside a training-only pipeline may be fine; if it depends on observed variables, use group-wise or model-based imputation; if it depends on the value itself (MNAR), the missingness is informative, so add an <code>income_missing</code> flag and avoid over-trusting imputed values. Fit imputers on training data only to avoid leakage, and monitor the missing rate in production.</p>\n","s":"Understand why it is missing (MCAR/MAR/MNAR), then impute appropriately and add a missing-indicator feature."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-eda~ca0_1","q":"EDA reveals 12 per cent missing in income. What do you do before imputing?","a":"<p>Check why: random, or related to other variables or the target (for example high earners skip)? The mechanism decides between imputing, adding a missing flag, or fixing collection.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-eda~ca0_2","q":"Why should EDA decisions use only training data?","a":"<p>Using the test set to choose features or transformations leaks information and makes the test score optimistic.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m3-eda~ca0_3","q":"Name three data-quality checks that take under a minute each.","a":"<p>Duplicate rows or keys, impossible values (negative, future dates), and null counts per column.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m3-eda~ta1_1","q":"<p><code>df.describe()</code> shows a <code>quantity</code> column with min −3, median 1, max 500, and <code>df.isna().mean()</code> shows 40% missing for <code>discount</code>. List three things you would check next and why.</p>\n","a":"<p>(1) Negative quantity: are these returns or refunds recorded as negative sales? If so, separate them; if not, they are errors. (2) Quantity 500 against a median of 1: a bulk or business order, or a typo? Look at the rows and decide whether they belong in the analysis. (3) 40% missing discount: does missing mean &quot;no discount&quot; (then fill with 0 and keep a flag), or &quot;not recorded&quot;? Check whether missingness depends on date or channel, which would suggest a logging change.</p>\n"}]}});